          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            U-duality is a kind of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+string+theory"} duality in string theory
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactifications"} KK-compactifications
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            to lower dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauged+supergravity"} gauged supergravity
            theories have global/local
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+groups"} gauge groups
            given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+real+forms"} split real forms
            of the
            = succeed "-series" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+Lie+groups"} exceptional Lie groups
          %p
            Here the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+Lie+groups"} exceptional Lie groups
            form a series
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E8"} E8
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E7"} E7
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E6"} E6
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 8
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 7
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 6
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E_8, E_7, E_6
          %p which is usefully thought of to continue as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 5
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi Spin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 10
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 4
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi SU
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 5
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 3
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi SU
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi SU
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E_5 \coloneqq Spin(10), E_4 \coloneqq SU(5), E_3 \coloneqq SU(3) \times SU(2)
                  \\,.
          %p
            (Notice that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_4
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 5
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_5
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 6
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_6
            are also the traditional choices for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenologically
            realistic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/grand+unified+theories"} grand unified theories
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+real+forms"} split real forms
            of this are traditionally written
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
